@noindent
And while typing in a note: @code{[[} starts a wikilink and opens
completion; @code{]]} closes it and rewrites it into canonical form, or
says why it cannot.  A link the index says is dead is coloured as broken
while you write it (@pxref{Seeing Them}).

@section The habit worth keeping

Rebuild the index (@kbd{C-c h i}) after adding or renaming notes.
Everything else reads from it, and nothing rebuilds it for you.

@node The Mode
@chapter The Mode

@cindex heroiclands-mode
@cindex minor mode
@findex heroiclands-mode
@findex global-heroiclands-mode

The buffer-local half of this package is a minor mode,
@code{heroiclands-mode}.  It carries the wikilink machinery and the
completions --- everything that is genuinely about @emph{this buffer}.
@kbd{C-h m} describes it wherever it is on, and the lighter @samp{HL} in
the mode line says when that is.

The @kbd{C-c h} prefix is @strong{not} part of it, and stays global on
purpose: jumping between repositories and grepping across all of them are
things you do from anywhere, including from a buffer belonging to no
project at all.

@section What the marker decides, and what the index decides

@cindex index, and what depends on it

Two different things gate this package, and it is worth keeping them
apart.

The @strong{project marker} decides that the mode is @emph{relevant}:
this is a content tree, so these commands mean something here.

The @strong{content index} decides which capabilities are @emph{live}.
Everything about wikilinks reads it, and without one they have nothing to
answer from:

@multitable @columnfractions .5 .5
@headitem Needs the index @tab Works without it
@item Completion after @code{[[} @tab Colouring wikilinks by part
@item Rewriting a link on @code{]]} @tab Content-table previews
@item @kbd{C-c h .} following a link @tab @kbd{C-c h i}, which builds one
@item Marking a link broken @tab The @kbd{C-c h} repository commands
@end multitable

@noindent
Each of those behaves differently without one, and deliberately so:

@multitable @columnfractions .3 .7
@item @kbd{C-c h .} @tab Refuses, naming the command that builds an index.
@item Completion @tab Offers nothing.  A completion function that raised an
error would be a completion function nobody could type through.
@item @code{]]} @tab Leaves the link as typed and @emph{says so}, once per
buffer.  Silence would be the worst outcome available: the author asked for
a check and would get neither the check nor a reason.
@item Colouring @tab Continues, minus the verdict.
@end multitable

So the mode still turns on without an index --- withholding it would take
away the previews and the colouring, which do not need one, and leave no
way to see why.  What it does instead is @strong{say so}: the lighter
reads @samp{HL?} rather than @samp{HL}, and enabling the mode in a buffer
with no index says once where to get one.

@kbd{C-c h i} then rebuilds it and re-examines every open content buffer,
so the lighter and the broken-link marking both catch up without
reopening anything.

@section It usually turns itself on

@code{global-heroiclands-mode} enables it in a buffer that is visiting a
file, in @code{markdown-mode} or @code{gfm-mode}, and inside a directory
tree holding a package-build configuration.

@vindex heroiclands-markers

That last test walks up from the file looking for any of the three names
package-build itself resolves, which is what @code{heroiclands-markers}
holds:

@verbatim
package-build.config.yaml
package-build.config.yml
package-build.config.mjs
@end verbatim

@noindent
Any one is enough.  The @file{.mjs} form is package-build's escape hatch
for a consumer that computes its configuration rather than declaring it;
two of them in one directory is an error, but that is package-build's
error to report rather than this package's to guess at.

That is the whole test, so a note in a new repository is covered the day
the repository exists.

@example
(global-heroiclands-mode 1)
@end example

@noindent
With that in your init file there is nothing to mark: open a content note
and the mode is on.

@section Marking a directory

The cleanest option for a tree that the project rule does not cover: a
@file{.dir-locals.el} in the directory.

@example
((markdown-mode . ((mode . heroiclands))))
@end example

Every markdown buffer at or below it gets the mode.  A @code{mode} entry
here is applied after the major mode is already set, so it does not
disturb it.

@section Marking a single file

A Local Variables block at the end of the file:

@example
<!-- Local Variables: -->
<!-- eval: (heroiclands-mode 1) -->
<!-- End: -->
@end example

Emacs looks for this in the last part of the file, and each line must sit
inside a comment --- hence the @samp{<!-- -->} wrappers.  This package
registers the form in @code{safe-local-eval-forms}, so Emacs applies it
without asking.

@section Two forms that look right and are not

@cindex frontmatter, and file-local variables

@strong{Not the first-line @samp{-*-} form.}  A content note's
frontmatter has to open the file: the @samp{---} must be on line one.  A
comment above it displaces the delimiter, and both @command{gray-matter}
and package-build's own parser then read the note as having @emph{no
frontmatter} --- so the build skips it, silently.  An editor convenience
is not worth the note.

@strong{Not @code{mode:} in the end-of-file block.}  A @code{mode:} entry
there is taken as the @emph{major} mode: Emacs enables the minor mode and
then leaves the buffer in @code{fundamental-mode}, losing markdown
entirely, and nothing says why.  Writing @code{mode: markdown} before it
does not help.  Use @code{eval:}, above.

@section Marking nothing at all

@kbd{M-x heroiclands-mode} toggles it in the current buffer.  Turning it
off removes the completions and the wikilink machinery from that buffer
and clears any content-table previews, which is the point of it being a
mode: a buffer where you want none of this can say so.

@node Starting Emacs
@chapter Starting Emacs

@cindex daemon
@cindex starting Emacs

Emacs runs as a @strong{daemon} started at login (@command{brew
services}, @code{emacs-plus@@31}).  Every route below attaches a frame to
that one session, so buffers, undo history, magit state and the warm
language server are all shared.

@multitable @columnfractions .22 .78
@item Login @tab Automatic. @code{brew services list} shows
@code{emacs-plus@@31 started}.
@item Dock or Spotlight @tab @strong{Emacs Client} in
@file{~/Applications} — not @emph{Emacs}, which starts a rival instance.
@item Terminal @tab @command{e} opens a file in the existing frame;
@command{ec} a new window; @command{et} a frame in this terminal.
@item git, @env{EDITOR} @tab Opens a frame in the current terminal.
Finish with @kbd{C-x #}.
@end multitable

@kbd{C-x C-c} closes the @emph{frame} and leaves the daemon running.
@kbd{C-u C-x C-c} (or @command{eq}) actually stops it.

@strong{Do not launch @file{Emacs.app} directly} — it starts a second,
independent Emacs that shares none of the above.

@section Why a daemon

Startup itself is only about half a second, so this is about @emph{state},
not speed.  What a cold frame rebuilds every time:

@multitable @columnfractions .5 .5
@item Link manifests (5,229 keys) @tab 0.06s
@item Handlebars helper scan @tab 2.06s
@item TypeScript language server indexing @tab 10–20s
@end multitable

